Task 1: Using ipa-healthcheck and Logs to Identify Issues
Subtask 1.1: Install and Configure ipa-healthcheck
First, let's ensure the ipa-healthcheck tool is available and properly configured.

Check if ipa-healthcheck is installed:
rpm -qa | grep ipa-healthcheck
Install ipa-healthcheck if not present:
sudo dnf install -y freeipa-healthcheck
Verify the installation:
ipa-healthcheck --version
Subtask 1.2: Run Initial Health Check
Execute a comprehensive health check:
sudo ipa-healthcheck --verbose
Run health check with JSON output for easier parsing:
sudo ipa-healthcheck --output-type json | python3 -m json.tool
Check specific health check sources:
# Check only DNS-related issues
sudo ipa-healthcheck --source ipahealthcheck.ipa.dna

# Check certificate issues
sudo ipa-healthcheck --source ipahealthcheck.ipa.certs

# Check replication issues
sudo ipa-healthcheck --source ipahealthcheck.ipa.replica
Subtask 1.3: Analyze IdM Log Files
Check the main IPA installation log:
sudo tail -f /var/log/ipaserver-install.log
Monitor real-time IdM service logs:
# Monitor Directory Server logs
sudo tail -f /var/log/dirsrv/slapd-*/access

# Monitor Kerberos logs
sudo tail -f /var/log/krb5kdc.log

# Monitor Apache logs
sudo tail -f /var/log/httpd/error_log
Search for specific error patterns:
# Search for authentication failures
sudo grep -i "authentication failed" /var/log/secure

# Search for LDAP connection issues
sudo grep -i "ldap" /var/log/messages

# Search for certificate errors
sudo grep -i "certificate" /var/log/httpd/error_log
Subtask 1.4: Create a Log Analysis Script
Create a script to automate log analysis:

cat > ~/idm_log_analyzer.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== IdM Log Analysis Report ==="
echo "Generated on: $(date)"
echo

echo "=== Recent Authentication Failures ==="
sudo grep -i "authentication failed" /var/log/secure | tail -10
echo

echo "=== LDAP Connection Issues ==="
sudo grep -i "ldap.*error\|ldap.*failed" /var/log/messages | tail -10
echo

echo "=== Certificate Warnings/Errors ==="
sudo grep -i "certificate.*error\|certificate.*warning" /var/log/httpd/error_log | tail -10
echo

echo "=== Kerberos Issues ==="
sudo grep -i "error\|failed" /var/log/krb5kdc.log | tail -10
echo

echo "=== Service Status ==="
systemctl status ipa --no-pager
EOF

chmod +x ~/idm_log_analyzer.sh
Run the log analysis script:
~/idm_log_analyzer.sh
Task 2: Troubleshoot Failed User Logins and Configuration Errors
Subtask 2.1: Simulate and Diagnose User Login Issues
Create a test user with intentional issues:
# Create a user
echo 'TempPassword123!' | kinit admin
ipa user-add testuser --first=Test --last=User --password
Simulate common login problems:
# Disable the user account
ipa user-disable testuser

# Try to authenticate as the disabled user
echo 'userpassword' | kinit testuser
Check user account status:
# Check user details
ipa user-show testuser

# Check if user is locked
ipa user-status testuser
Subtask 2.2: Troubleshoot Password Policy Issues
Check current password policies:
# View global password policy
ipa pwpolicy-show

# Check user-specific password policy
ipa pwpolicy-show --user=testuser
Create a script to diagnose password issues:
cat > ~/password_troubleshoot.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

USER=$1
if [ -z "$USER" ]; then
    echo "Usage: $0 <username>"
    exit 1
fi

echo "=== Password Troubleshooting for $USER ==="
echo

echo "User Status:"
ipa user-status $USER 2>/dev/null || echo "User not found or access denied"
echo

echo "Password Policy:"
ipa pwpolicy-show --user=$USER 2>/dev/null || echo "Cannot retrieve password policy"
echo

echo "Recent Authentication Attempts:"
sudo grep "$USER" /var/log/secure | tail -5
EOF

chmod +x ~/password_troubleshoot.sh
Use the troubleshooting script:
~/password_troubleshoot.sh testuser
Subtask 2.3: Fix Configuration Errors
Check and fix IdM service configuration:
# Verify IPA configuration
sudo ipa-server-install --unattended --setup-dns --auto-forwarders --dry-run

# Check for configuration inconsistencies
sudo ipa-replica-manage list-ruv
Repair common configuration issues:
# Re-enable the test user
ipa user-enable testuser

# Reset user password
ipa passwd testuser

# Unlock user account if locked
ipa user-unlock testuser
Task 3: Resolve Issues with DNS, Kerberos, and SSSD
Subtask 3.1: Diagnose and Fix DNS Issues
Test DNS resolution:
# Test forward DNS resolution
nslookup $(hostname)

# Test reverse DNS resolution
nslookup $(hostname -I | awk '{print $1}')

# Test SRV records
dig _ldap._tcp.$(dnsdomainname) SRV
dig _kerberos._tcp.$(dnsdomainname) SRV
Create a DNS diagnostic script:
cat > ~/dns_diagnostics.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

DOMAIN=$(dnsdomainname)
HOSTNAME=$(hostname)
IP=$(hostname -I | awk '{print $1}')

echo "=== DNS Diagnostics for IdM ==="
echo "Domain: $DOMAIN"
echo "Hostname: $HOSTNAME"
echo "IP Address: $IP"
echo

echo "=== Forward DNS Resolution ==="
nslookup $HOSTNAME
echo

echo "=== Reverse DNS Resolution ==="
nslookup $IP
echo

echo "=== SRV Records Check ==="
echo "LDAP SRV:"
dig _ldap._tcp.$DOMAIN SRV +short
echo "Kerberos SRV:"
dig _kerberos._tcp.$DOMAIN SRV +short
echo "Kerberos UDP SRV:"
dig _kerberos._udp.$DOMAIN SRV +short
echo

echo "=== DNS Server Status ==="
systemctl status named-pkcs11 --no-pager
EOF

chmod +x ~/dns_diagnostics.sh
Run DNS diagnostics:
~/dns_diagnostics.sh
Fix common DNS issues:
# Restart DNS service
sudo systemctl restart named-pkcs11

# Check DNS configuration
sudo named-checkconf

# Verify zone files
sudo named-checkzone $(dnsdomainname) /var/named/dynamic/$(dnsdomainname).db
Subtask 3.2: Troubleshoot Kerberos Issues
Check Kerberos configuration:
# View Kerberos configuration
cat /etc/krb5.conf

# Check KDC status
sudo systemctl status krb5kdc --no-pager
Test Kerberos authentication:
# Destroy existing tickets
kdestroy

# Test kinit with admin user
echo 'admin_password' | kinit admin

# List current tickets
klist

# Test service tickets
kvno HTTP/$(hostname)
Create Kerberos troubleshooting script:
cat > ~/kerberos_troubleshoot.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== Kerberos Troubleshooting ==="
echo

echo "=== Current Kerberos Tickets ==="
klist 2>/dev/null || echo "No current tickets"
echo

echo "=== Kerberos Configuration ==="
echo "Default realm: $(grep default_realm /etc/krb5.conf | awk '{print $3}')"
echo "KDC servers:"
grep kdc /etc/krb5.conf | head -3
echo

echo "=== KDC Service Status ==="
systemctl status krb5kdc --no-pager
echo

echo "=== Recent Kerberos Errors ==="
sudo tail -10 /var/log/krb5kdc.log | grep -i error
EOF

chmod +x ~/kerberos_troubleshoot.sh
Run Kerberos diagnostics:
~/kerberos_troubleshoot.sh
Subtask 3.3: Resolve SSSD Issues
Check SSSD configuration and status:
# Check SSSD configuration
sudo cat /etc/sssd/sssd.conf

# Check SSSD service status
sudo systemctl status sssd --no-pager

# Check SSSD domains
sudo sssctl domain-list
Test SSSD functionality:
# Test user lookup
getent passwd testuser

# Test group lookup
getent group ipausers

# Check SSSD cache
sudo sssctl user-checks testuser
Create SSSD diagnostic script:
cat > ~/sssd_diagnostics.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== SSSD Diagnostics ==="
echo

echo "=== SSSD Service Status ==="
systemctl status sssd --no-pager
echo

echo "=== SSSD Domains ==="
sudo sssctl domain-list
echo

echo "=== SSSD Configuration Summary ==="
sudo grep -E "^\[|^domains|^services" /etc/sssd/sssd.conf
echo

echo "=== Test User Resolution ==="
getent passwd admin 2>/dev/null || echo "Cannot resolve admin user"
echo

echo "=== SSSD Cache Status ==="
sudo sssctl cache-expire --everything 2>/dev/null && echo "Cache cleared" || echo "Cache clear failed"
echo

echo "=== Recent SSSD Logs ==="
sudo journalctl -u sssd --no-pager -n 10
EOF

chmod +x ~/sssd_diagnostics.sh
Run SSSD diagnostics:
~/sssd_diagnostics.sh
Fix common SSSD issues:
# Clear SSSD cache
sudo sssctl cache-expire --everything

# Restart SSSD service
sudo systemctl restart sssd

# Check SSSD logs for errors
sudo journalctl -u sssd -f
Subtask 3.4: Comprehensive System Health Check
Create a comprehensive troubleshooting script:
cat > ~/idm_comprehensive_check.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "=========================================="
echo "    IdM Comprehensive Health Check"
echo "=========================================="
echo "Generated on: $(date)"
echo

# Function to check service status
check_service() {
    local service=$1
    echo -n "Checking $service: "
    if systemctl is-active $service >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "RUNNING"
    else
        echo "STOPPED/FAILED"
        systemctl status $service --no-pager -l
    fi
}

echo "=== Service Status Check ==="
check_service ipa
check_service named-pkcs11
check_service krb5kdc
check_service sssd
check_service httpd
check_service dirsrv@$(hostname -d | tr '.' '-' | tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]').service
echo

echo "=== Network Connectivity ==="
echo -n "Hostname resolution: "
nslookup $(hostname) >/dev/null 2>&1 && echo "OK" || echo "FAILED"
echo -n "Reverse DNS: "
nslookup $(hostname -I | awk '{print $1}') >/dev/null 2>&1 && echo "OK" || echo "FAILED"
echo

echo "=== Certificate Status ==="
echo -n "HTTP certificate: "
echo | openssl s_client -connect $(hostname):443 2>/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -dates 2>/dev/null && echo "OK" || echo "FAILED"
echo

echo "=== Authentication Test ==="
echo -n "Kerberos ticket: "
klist >/dev/null 2>&1 && echo "VALID" || echo "NO TICKET"
echo

echo "=== Running ipa-healthcheck ==="
sudo ipa-healthcheck --failures-only 2>/dev/null || echo "ipa-healthcheck not available or failed"
echo

echo "=========================================="
echo "Health check completed"
echo "=========================================="
EOF

chmod +x ~/idm_comprehensive_check.sh
Run the comprehensive health check:
~/idm_comprehensive_check.sh
Troubleshooting Tips and Common Issues
Common Authentication Issues
Issue: Users cannot authenticate Solution:

# Check if user exists and is enabled
ipa user-show username
ipa user-enable username

# Check password policy
ipa pwpolicy-show --user=username

# Clear SSSD cache
sudo sssctl cache-expire --everything
Common DNS Issues
Issue: DNS resolution fails Solution:

# Check DNS service
sudo systemctl restart named-pkcs11

# Verify DNS configuration
sudo named-checkconf

# Test DNS resolution
nslookup $(hostname)
Common Kerberos Issues
Issue: Kerberos authentication fails Solution:

# Check time synchronization
sudo chrony sources

# Restart Kerberos services
sudo systemctl restart krb5kdc

# Test with kinit
kdestroy && kinit admin
Common SSSD Issues
Issue: User lookup fails Solution:

# Restart SSSD
sudo systemctl restart sssd

# Clear cache
sudo sssctl cache-expire --everything

# Check configuration
sudo sssctl config-check
Conclusion
In this lab, you have learned essential troubleshooting skills for Identity Management systems. You have:

Mastered diagnostic tools: Used ipa-healthcheck to identify system health issues and analyzed various log files to pinpoint problems
Resolved authentication issues: Diagnosed and fixed user login problems, password policy issues, and account lockouts
Fixed service problems: Troubleshot DNS resolution, Kerberos authentication, and SSSD functionality
Developed systematic approaches: Created reusable scripts for ongoing monitoring and troubleshooting
These skills are crucial for maintaining a healthy IdM environment in production systems. The systematic approach you've learned - using health checks, analyzing logs, and testing individual components - will help you quickly identify and resolve issues in real-world scenarios.

Why This Matters: Identity Management is critical infrastructure for organizations. When IdM fails, users cannot access resources, applications break, and productivity suffers. The troubleshooting skills you've developed ensure you can maintain high availability and quickly restore service when issues occur.

Remember to regularly run health checks, monitor logs proactively, and maintain documentation of common issues and their solutions for your specific environment.
